.TH FMT 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fmt \- simple text formatter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fmt
.RB [ \-cmnps ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR chars ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR num ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR num ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width " |"
.B \-g
.IR goal ]
.RI [ goal " [" maximum ]]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fmt
fills and joins lines of text into paragraphs no wider than a target
width (65 columns by default) and writes the result to standard
output.
Input is read from each
.I file
in turn, or from standard input if none is given or for a
.I file
of
.BR \- .
All input is read before any output is produced, and the files are
formatted as one continuous text.
.PP
Tabs in the input are expanded to spaces (every 8 columns, or
.B \-l
columns); the output never contains tabs.
.PP
A paragraph is a run of non-blank lines.
Blank lines are preserved.
Words are packed greedily: as many words as fit within the width go on
each line, and a word longer than the width is placed on a line by
itself.
Words are separated by one space, or by two spaces after a word that
ends a sentence: one whose last character, ignoring closing
.BR ) ,
.BR ] ,
.BR \(dq ,
and
.BR \(aq ,
is one of
.B .!?\&
(or the
.B \-d
set).
.PP
Every output line of a paragraph is indented like the paragraph's
first input line, unless
.B \-c
is given.
Lines whose first non-blank character is
.B .\&
(for example troff requests) are copied unchanged and end the paragraph, unless
.B \-n
is given.
.PP
If the first operand (after options) consists only of digits and
neither
.B \-w
nor
.B \-g
was given, it sets the width; a second all-digit operand, the BSD
.IR maximum ,
is accepted and ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-crown\-margin
Crown margin: indent the first output line of a paragraph like the
first input line, and the remaining lines like the second input line.
.TP
.B \-m
Copy lines that look like mail headers (a letter followed by letters,
digits, or hyphens and then a colon) unchanged; they also end the
paragraph.
.TP
.B \-n
Also format lines beginning with
.BR . .
.TP
.B \-p
Start a new paragraph whenever the indentation changes.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-split\-only
Split long lines but never join short ones: each input line is
formatted on its own.
.TP
.BI \-d " chars"
Use the characters in
.I chars
as the sentence-ending set instead of
.BR .!? .
.TP
.BI \-l " num"
Expand input tabs to every
.I num
columns (default 8; values below 1 mean 8).
.TP
.BI \-t " num"
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width= \fIwidth\fP
Set the maximum line width.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-goal= \fIgoal\fP
Same as
.BR \-w ;
there is no separate goal width.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid usage, a width below 1, out of memory, or a
.I file
that could not be opened (the remaining files are still formatted).
.SH EXAMPLES
Reformat a file to 72 columns:
.PP
.EX
$ fmt \-w 72 notes.txt
.EE
.PP
Rewrap a mail message, keeping its headers:
.PP
.EX
$ fmt \-m < message
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pr (1),
.BR vi (1)
.SH STANDARDS
.B fmt
is not specified by POSIX.
The options follow BSD
.B fmt
and GNU coreutils.
.SH BUGS
Because all files are formatted as a single text, a paragraph at the
end of one file can be joined with one at the start of the next.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
lines beginning with
.B .
and mail header lines are not protected, and
.B \-n
and
.B \-m
have no effect.
.PP
With
.BR \-m ,
header lines are recognised anywhere in the text, not only at the top
of a message.
.PP
Numeric option arguments are parsed without error checking, so
trailing garbage is ignored and a non-numeric
.B \-w
or
.B \-g
argument is reported only as a width below 1.
